    "message": {
     "content": "### Offer Assessment & True Compensation\n\nAt first glance, an annualized comparison looks modest:\n* **Current Meta IC6:** ~$526,000/yr (Base: $240k | Bonus: $36k | RSUs: ~$250k run-rate)\n* **Google L6 Offer (Annualized average):** ~$552,000/yr (Base: $235k | Bonus target: $47k | RSUs: $270k/yr)\n\nHowever, **Google does not vest equity evenly.** Google standardly uses a front-loaded schedule (typically **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%** or **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%**, vesting monthly):\n\n| Year | Vest % (38/32/20/10) | GSU Value | Base + Target Bonus (20%) | Total Compensation (Pre-Refresher) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Year 1** | 38% | $410,400 | $282,000 | **~$692,400** |\n| **Year 2** | 32% | $345,600 | $282,000 | **~$627,600** + Year 1 Refresher |\n| **Year 3** | 20% | $216,000 | $282,000 | **~$498,000** + Stacking Refreshers |\n| **Year 4** | 10% | $108,000 | $282,000 | **~$390,000** + Stacking Refreshers |\n\n*(If they use the 33/33/22/12 schedule, Year 1 and Year 2 total comp will both sit around **~$638,400** before refreshers).*\n\n---\n\n### Is it a good offer?\n\n**It is a solid, market-standard initial offer for an L6 PM, but it is not top-of-band.** \n\n* **The Equity ($1.08M) is strong:** For an initial offer without a bidding war, a $1.08M initial grant is competitive for L6 PM.\n* **The Base Salary is low:** You are being asked to take a base pay cut ($235k vs $240k at Meta), even though Google\u2019s posted band goes up to **$271k**.\n* **$0 Sign-On Bonus is an oversight:** Offering zero sign-on to a passive candidate walking away from an established trajectory and unvested equity at Meta is standard recruiter opening posture.\n\n---\n\n### Recommendation: Negotiate (Do not accept as-is, do not walk away yet)\n\nBecause Google approached you and you are happy at Meta, **you hold all the leverage.** Google views Meta IC6 as a prime lateral hire and expects candidates at this level to negotiate.\n\n---\n\n### Negotiation Strategy: What & How Much to Ask For\n\nYou should negotiate three specific levers:\n\n#### 1. Base Salary: Ask for $255,000 \u2013 $260,000\n* **Rationale:** You should not take a base salary cut to move laterally from Meta. Google\u2019s posted band for this role tops out at $271,000, so $255,000\u2013$260,000 is well within range.\n\n#### 2. Sign-On Bonus: Ask for $75,000 \u2013 $100,000\n* **Rationale:** Google frequently uses sign-on bonuses to bridge the transition and offset unvested equity or impending bonus payouts left behind at competitor firms. A $75k\u2013$100k sign-on bonus for an L6 PM is standard in lateral FAANG moves.\n\n#### 3. Equity (GSUs): Ask for $1,200,000 \u2013 $1,250,000\n* **Rationale:** Mention that your long-term earnings trajectory and refresher stacking at Meta make you cautious about the Year 3/4 drop-off in Google's front-loaded equity model. Increasing the grant to ~$1.25M brings Year 1 comp above $750k and cushions the back half of the 4-year cycle.\n\n---\n\n### Sample Script for Your Recruiter\n\n> *\"Thank you again for the offer. I really enjoyed meeting the team, and I\u2019m excited about the scope of the role. As you know, I was not actively looking and am very well-positioned at Meta, so compensation is a key factor in deciding whether making a move makes sense right now.*\n>\n> *Looking closely at the numbers:*\n> 1. *My current base salary at Meta is $240,000, and given the posted range goes up to $271,000, I\u2019d like to see the base salary at **$255,000**.*\n> 2. *To offset the unvested equity and upcoming bonus cycle I\u2019d be walking away from, I\u2019d be looking for a sign-on bonus of **$100,000**.*\n> 3. *Considering the front-loaded structure and the drop in Year 3/4 equity before refreshers fully stack, increasing the initial grant to **$1,250,000** would make this an easy decision for me.*\n>\n> *If we can get to these numbers, I\u2019m ready to sign.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Summary Checklist Before Signing\n1. **Clarify the exact GSU vest schedule:** Confirm whether the offer uses the 38/32/20/10 or 33/33/22/12 split, and whether it vests monthly or quarterly.\n2. **Weigh Year 3+ risk:** Google refreshers vest at 25%/year and stack, but your performance rating dictates the size. A larger initial grant and sign-on bonus guarantee your downside is protected regardless of future refresh cycles.",
